<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="S-methyl-5-thio-α-D-ribose 1-phosphate degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6755" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Polyamines, organic molecules with multiple amino groups, are critical for proper regulation of the cell cycle, and are synthesized in large amounts in rapidly growing cells. During polyamine synthesis |FRAME: MET L-methionine| is consumed through the utilization of |FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-adenosyl-L-methionine| (SAM), in a reaction that releases |FRAME:5-METHYLTHIOADENOSINE &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-methyl-5&apos;-thioadenosine| (MTA) (see the pathway |FRAME:BSUBPOLYAMSYN-PWY spermidine biosynthesis I| for an example). MTA is a strong inhibitor of polyamine biosynthesis and transmethylation reactions, and its concentration is tightly regulated. This control is achieved through the |FRAME: PWY-4361 methionine salvage I (bacteria and plants)| pathway, where MTA is recycled through a series of reactions that convert its 5-methylthio-D-ribose moiety to |FRAME: MET L-methionine|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; This |FRAME: PWY-4361 &quot;methionine salvage&quot;| pathway is found in a wide variety of organisms, including bacteria, protozoa, plants and mammals. This pathway has been partially characterized for a number of organisms, including rat |CITS: [7852397][7068632][7007366]|, plants, yeast |CITS: [3906034]|, bacteria and protozoal parasites |CITS: [8585735][ 11443076]|. The best characterized pathway is from the Gram-negative bacterium |FRAME: TAX-573 &lt;i&gt;Klebsiella pneumoniae&lt;/i&gt;| |CITS:[8694776][11371200][9880484][7852397][8227039]|. The initial steps of the methionine salvage pathway are different between microorganisms (such as the bacteria |FRAME: TAX-573 &lt;i&gt;Klebsiella pneumoniae&lt;/i&gt;| |CITS: [8694776]| and |FRAME: TAX-1423 &lt;i&gt;Bacillus subtilis&lt;/i&gt;| |CITS: [12022921]|), protozoa (such as |FRAME: TAX-5741 &lt;i&gt;Giardia intestinalis&lt;/i&gt;| and |FRAME: TAX-5833 &lt;i&gt;Plasmodium falciparum&lt;/i&gt;| |CITS: [2854458]|) and mammalian cells. The different pathways that describe these initial steps are described under |FRAME: Methylthioadenosine-Degradation &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-methyl-5&apos;-thioadenosine Degradation|.</Description>
